<!-- 2026-11-14 (John): μr help. Table = values John found by web search (Engineering Toolbox / Lucefin); the
     book references are general sources on permeability, NOT checked value-by-value against this table. -->
<div class="hp-modal" id="muHelp" onclick="if(event.target===this)this.style.display='none'"><div class="hp-box">
  <h3>Steel relative permeability μr</h3>
  <p>μr = μ / μ₀, with μ₀ = 4π×10⁻⁷ H/m. In steel μr is not a constant: it changes non-linearly with the applied field H and saturation, and with alloy, heat treatment and manufacturing.
  In the Lalji (2020) Eq. 3 pole strength, Q = (μr−1)·B<sub>ax</sub>·A / (1 + N(μr−1)), a long part (small N) is very sensitive to μr: for N ≈ 0.001, μr = 80 gives about 1/12 of the μr = ∞ value.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>μr (typical)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Electrical steel</td><td>≈ 4 000 (higher depending on grade and flux density)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Carbon steel</td><td>≈ 100 – 600+ (carbon content, field strength)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ferritic stainless steel (annealed)</td><td>≈ 1 000 – 1 800</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martensitic stainless steel (annealed)</td><td>≈ 750 – 950</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Martensitic stainless steel (hardened)</td><td>≈ 40 – 95</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Austenitic stainless steel (304, 316)</td><td>≈ 1.003 – 7 (classed non-magnetic; cold work can raise it)</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p class="muted">MTX default μr = 80. The Earth's field is weak (about 40 A/m), so the relevant value is the low-field (initial) permeability of the actual collar steel, which is not published for drilling components.</p>
  <div class="ref"><b>References</b><br>
    Table values: Engineering Toolbox, "Permeability"; Lucefin, "Magnetic permeability" (web, via search, 2026-11-14).<br>
    Definition and behaviour of permeability: Cullity &amp; Graham, <i>Introduction to Magnetic Materials</i>, 2nd ed., Wiley-IEEE (2009); Jiles, <i>Introduction to Magnetism and Magnetic Materials</i>, CRC Press.<br>
    Pole strength: Lalji, Haneef, Khan &amp; Ali (2020), J. Petrol. Explor. Prod. Technol. 10:821–828, Eqs. 3–4.<br>
    Demagnetising factor: Osborn (1945), "Demagnetizing factors of the general ellipsoid", Phys. Rev. 67:351.</div>
  <p style="text-align:right;margin:12px 0 0"><button type="button" onclick="document.getElementById('muHelp').style.display='none'">Close</button></p>
</div></div>
<script>function openMuHelp(){ document.getElementById('muHelp').style.display = 'flex'; }</script>
